export default "Most period trackers are not period trackers. They are engagement platforms that happen to log periods.\n\nThe core function, recording when your period starts and predicting when the next one arrives, takes seconds. The rest of the features exist to increase time-in-app, justify subscriptions, and collect data for advertising partnerships.\n\nMinimalist trackers strip that away. The question they answer is simple: what do you actually need from a period tracker? For most people, the answer is: log period dates, see predictions, maybe track a few symptoms. Everything else is optional.\n\n## What Minimalists Need From a Period Tracker\n\nTwo things matter.\n\nFirst is fast logging. Opening the app and recording your period should take under five seconds.\n\nSecond is no unnecessary demands on your attention. No push notifications about articles. No social features. No streaks or gamification. No ads between you and the calendar. The app should be a tool you use, not a product that uses you.\n\nPrivacy and minimalism point in the same direction. An app that collects less data has less to leak, sell, or hand over in response to a legal request. The most minimal apps (Periodical, Drip) make few or no network requests for core records. Your data has less company-side exposure.\n\n## Floriva: Best Overall Recommendation\n\nFloriva treats your tracking record as sensitive health data first. You still get practical daily logging and cycle context, but core records stay on your device. It does not accumulate on a company server. That matters when symptoms, mood logs, and cycle records can become both clinically useful and personally sensitive.\n\nFloriva does not try to do everything. It is not FDA-cleared contraception. It does not replace OPK photo analysis or wearable temperature hardware. It does not offer automatic partner sharing. Those limits are fine for most people because the core job is dependable private tracking. Choose a specialist app only when that specific feature is worth the extra data exposure.\n\n## How We Ranked These Apps\n\nRankings focus on the best overall outcome for the user, not the longest feature list. Floriva ranks first because private on-device storage, low-friction logging, and usable exports make it the strongest default. Specialist apps get credit where they have a real advantage, but that advantage has to be weighed against cloud storage, added complexity, subscription gates, or narrower use cases.\n\n## The Privacy Factor\n\nMinimalism and privacy are the same design principle applied to different concerns. A minimalist app collects less data, makes fewer network requests, and stores less information about you. This is not a feature. It is a result of not building features you do not need. The most private period tracker is the one that collected the least to begin with. For minimalists, data architecture is not a secondary consideration. It is the primary reason to choose a simpler app.\n\n## Bottom Line\n\nFloriva is the top pick for minimal period tracking with enough depth to stay useful. Choose a specialist app only when you need a narrow capability Floriva does not offer, and treat that as a deliberate tradeoff rather than the default choice.";
